  "summary": "Shoulder dystocia: call for help, McRoberts + suprapubic (NOT fundal) pressure first, then internal rotation / deliver the posterior arm / all-fours. Cord prolapse: elevate the presenting part continuously and go to an emergent cesarean — do not replace the cord. Abruption: painful bleeding + a rigid tender uterus + fetal distress ± DIC.",
  "whyThisMatters": "Shoulder dystocia, cord prolapse and abruption each have one wrong move that makes them worse — fundal pressure, replacing the cord, delaying delivery — and each is a scripted, time-critical drill where hesitation costs the baby.",
  "clinicalTakeaway": "Shoulder dystocia: McRoberts + suprapubic (never fundal) pressure, then internal rotation / posterior arm / all-fours. Cord prolapse: elevate the presenting part continuously and go to an emergent cesarean — do not replace the cord. Abruption: painful bleeding + rigid tender uterus + fetal distress — deliver, and anticipate DIC.",
  "body": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "Shoulder dystocia — HELPERR"
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "tone": "danger",
      "text": "Time-critical — aim to deliver within ~5 minutes of recognition (rising risk of hypoxic injury and brachial plexus injury). Note the time. Do NOT apply fundal pressure and do NOT apply excessive downward traction on the head."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "H — Help: call additional OB, NICU / pediatrics, and anesthesia NOW.",
        "E — Evaluate for episiotomy: only if needed for room to perform internal maneuvers (it does not relieve a bony impaction).",
        "L — Legs: McRoberts — hyperflex the maternal thighs onto the abdomen (flattens the lumbar lordosis, rotates the symphysis). Resolves ~40% alone.",
        "P — suprapubic Pressure: continuous or rocking, directed downward and laterally onto the fetal anterior shoulder — NOT fundal pressure.",
        "E — Enter (internal rotation): Rubin II (pressure on the posterior aspect of the anterior shoulder → adduction), then add Woods screw (rotate the posterior shoulder anteriorly) or reverse Woods screw.",
        "R — Remove the posterior arm: sweep it across the fetal chest and deliver the hand / forearm (highly effective).",
        "R — Roll: the Gaskin all-fours maneuver (mother onto hands and knees), then reattempt delivery of the posterior (now uppermost) shoulder.",
        "Last resort: deliberate clavicle fracture; Zavanelli maneuver (cephalic replacement → cesarean); symphysiotomy."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "diagram": {
        "title": "Shoulder dystocia — the sequence, on a clock",
        "caption": "About 30 seconds per maneuver; move on rather than repeating what has failed, and repeat the whole sequence with fresh hands if the first pass fails. The measured fall in cord arterial pH is about 0.011 per minute (Leung 2011) — roughly a quarter of the 0.04 per minute long taught from an older theoretical estimate — and the risk of hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy is low with a head-to-body interval under 5 minutes, then rises. The honest reading is urgency without panic: there is time to work the sequence properly, and hard traction is what injures the plexus. Posterior-arm delivery reduces the obstructing shoulder diameter more than any other maneuver (Poggi 2003).",
